<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-vector-geom">
<title>Reflect on your understanding</title>

<introduction><p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-vector-geom-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand each of the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing them to memory.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>
	<title>Vector norm in <m> \R^2 </m></title>
	<p>
	For vectors in <m> \R^2 </m>,
	explain the connection between decomposing a vector as a
	<term><xref ref="definition-vectors-linear-combination" text="custom">linear combination</xref></term>
	of the <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-std-basis" text="title" /></term>,
	the Pythagorean Theorm,
	and vector norm.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Unit vectors and normalization</title>
	<p> State the definition of <term>unit vector</term> and explain how to create a unit vector parallel to a given vector. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Distance</title>
	<p> Explain how a vector norm can be used to determine the distance between two points or between two vectors. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Dot product as algebra-geometry bridge</title>
	<p> State the connection between the dot product, vector norm, and angle between vectors. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Norm as dot product</title>
	<p> State how a vector norm can be computed using the dot product. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Dot product as matrix product</title>
	<p> State how the dot product can be computed as a matrix product of column vectors. </p>
</exercise>

</reading-questions>
